readme.api -- The configuration interface for modules
=====================================================

How the core and the modules get at the configuration.  The declarations
are in include/conffile.h; the implementation is in src/conffile.c (the
tree, the lookups and the binding of directive tables), src/conf_lexer.l
(the flex scanner) and src/conf_parser.y (the bison grammar).  The syntax of
the file itself is in readme.config.

1. How it works
---------------

The grammar knows nothing of the settings: it accepts any block, entry and
include, and builds a tree of them.  Settings are checked when a table of
directives is bound to a block of the tree -- by the core for its own
blocks, by the module loader for each module's block -- so a module can
define any setting without touching the parser.

    start-up                              REHASH
    --------                              ------
    conf_load("ircservices.conf")         conf_load(...)      new tree
    core tables bound (READ)              core tables bound (READ)
    conf_commit()                         conf_commit()      old tree freed
    core tables set (SET)                 core tables set (SET)
    for each loadmodule:                  "reconfigure" callback (0)
        dlopen, module_config bound       every module_config bound (READ)
        (READ+SET), init_module()         every module_config set (SET)
                                          "reconfigure" callback (1)

If the file has a syntax error, or a core table fails to bind, the new tree
is discarded (conf_discard()) and the running configuration is untouched.

The tree is parsed before the first module is loaded, so it is complete
and available from the moment a module's init_module() runs -- and even
before, since the module's directive table is bound first.

2. Declaring settings: module_config
------------------------------------

The usual way for a module to get its settings is a table of
ConfigDirective called `module_config`.  The loader finds it by name, binds
it to every `module "<this module>"` block before calling init_module(),
binds it again on every REHASH, and restores the previous values of the
variables (deconfigure()) when the module is unloaded.

    static char *s_MyServ, *desc_MyServ;
    static int32 MyMax;
    static time_t MyDelay;
    static int MyFlag;

    ConfigDirective module_config[] = {
        { "MyServName", { { CD_STRING, CF_DIRREQ, &s_MyServ },
                          { CD_STRING, 0, &desc_MyServ } } },
        { "MyMax",      { { CD_POSINT, 0, &MyMax } } },
        { "MyDelay",    { { CD_TIME, 0, &MyDelay } } },
        { "MyFlag",     { { CD_SET, 0, &MyFlag } } },
        { NULL }
    };

which binds

    module "misc/myserv" {
        MyServName = "MyServ", "My Service";
        MyMax = 10;
        MyDelay = 5m;
        MyFlag;
    };

Each directive has up to CONFIG_MAXPARAMS (8) parameters; the values of an
entry fill them in order.

Parameter types:

    CD_INT       int32; any integer (decimal, 0octal, 0xhex)
    CD_POSINT    int32; an integer > 0
    CD_PORT      int32; 1..65535
    CD_STRING    char *; a copy of the value, freed by the binding code
    CD_TIME      time_t; a time ("90", "1h30m")
    CD_TIMEMSEC  int32 in milliseconds; seconds with up to 3 decimals
    CD_SET       int; set to 1 when the entry is present (a flag).  It
                 takes a value only if that value is yes/no/on/off/
                 true/false; a false value sets 0 and skips the
                 remaining parameters of the directive.  A flag followed
                 by other parameters (e.g. KillClonesAutokill = 30m;) sets
                 1 and passes the values on to them.
    CD_FUNC      a function called with each value (see below)
    CD_DEPRECATED
                 accepted with a warning, value ignored

Flags:

    CF_DIRREQ    (on the first parameter) the directive is required; if no
                 block has it, binding fails
    CF_OPTIONAL  the value may be omitted (the variable keeps its previous
                 value); following parameters should be optional too
    CF_MULTI     (on a trailing CD_FUNC) the function is called for every
                 remaining value: `AllowHost = /a, /b, /c;`

Parameters that are not given leave their variables alone; a directive
given several times keeps the last values, except for CD_FUNC, which is
called for every occurrence (that is how list settings accumulate).

CD_FUNC handlers have the signature

    int handler(const char *filename, int linenum, char *param);

- with filename != NULL, one value is being read: validate `param` (a
  private, modifiable copy), store it in a pending ("new") variable, and
  return 1, or report with config_error(filename, linenum, ...) and return
  0;
- with filename == NULL, `linenum` is the action: CDFUNC_INIT (a read is
  starting: clear the pending data), CDFUNC_SET (make the pending data
  current) or CDFUNC_DECONFIG (the module is going away: drop the current
  data).  The return value is ignored.

The read/set split is what makes a REHASH atomic: values are read into
pending storage for every table first, and only copied into the variables
once everything was read successfully.  See do_RejectEmail() or
do_LoadModule() in src/init.c for complete handlers.

3. Reading the tree directly
----------------------------

A module may also look at its block directly, at any time: from
init_module(), from a command handler, from a callback.  This is the way
to go for structured settings (nested blocks), for settings whose meaning
depends on other settings, or for optional features that do not deserve a
variable.

    #include "conffile.h"

    const ConfNode *me = conf_this_module();   /* module "<this module>" */

    const char *greeting = conf_get_string(me, "Greeting", "Hello!");
    int32 max = conf_get_int(me, "MaxEntries", 32);
    time_t delay = conf_get_time(me, "Delay", 60);
    int verbose = conf_get_bool(me, "Verbose", 0);

Every function accepts a NULL node and then finds nothing or returns the
default, so the calls above (and below) are safe even if there is no
block at all.  A value of the
wrong type is reported (with its file and line) and the default returned.

Repeated entries and nested blocks:

    module "misc/myserv" {
        Channel = "#help";
        Channel = "#support", "#staff";
        listen {
            address = 127.0.0.1;
            port = 8080;
        };
        listen {
            address = ::1;
            port = 8080;
        };
    };

    const ConfNode *e, *b;
    int i;

    for (e = conf_first_entry(me, "Channel"); e;
         e = conf_next_entry(e, "Channel")) {
        for (i = 0; i < conf_value_count(e); i++)
            join_channel(conf_value(e, i));
    }
    for (b = conf_find_block(me, "listen", NULL); b;
         b = conf_next_block(b, "listen", NULL)) {
        open_listener(conf_get_string(b, "address", "0.0.0.0"),
                      conf_get_int(b, "port", 80));
    }

Reference (include/conffile.h):

    const ConfNode *conf_root(void);
        The top level of the configuration.
    const ConfNode *conf_find_block(parent, name, label);
    const ConfNode *conf_next_block(block, name, label);
        Blocks called `name` directly inside `parent` (conf_root() for the
        top level), with label `label` (any label if NULL).
    const ConfNode *conf_module_block(modulename);
    conf_this_module()
        The (first) `module "<name>"` block; the macro gives the calling
        module's own.
    const ConfNode *conf_find_entry(block, key);
        The last entry `key` in `block` (the one in effect).
    const ConfNode *conf_first_entry(block, key);
    const ConfNode *conf_next_entry(entry, key);
        Every entry `key` (every entry if NULL), in file order.
    int conf_value_count(entry);
    const char *conf_value(entry, index);
        The values of an entry, as strings, quotes removed.
    conf_get_string / conf_get_int / conf_get_time / conf_get_bool
        The first value of entry `key`, converted, or a default.
    int conf_parse_bool(s);
        1 for yes/on/true, 0 for no/off/false, -1 otherwise.
    void config_error(filename, linenum, fmt, ...);
        Report a problem, to the log and (at start-up) the terminal; use
        node->file and node->line to point at the offending entry.

ConfNode is declared in the header (type, name, label, values, file, line,
children, next, parent) and may be read directly, but never modified.

Lifetime.  Nodes belong to the tree, and a REHASH replaces the tree: do not
keep ConfNode pointers across a REHASH.  Look them up again when you need
them, or copy what you need, typically from the "reconfigure" callback,
which is called with 0 before the modules' tables are rebound and with 1
after; the new tree is already in place in both calls:

    static int do_reconfigure(int after_configure)
    {
        if (after_configure)
            reload_listeners(conf_this_module());
        return 0;
    }
    ...
    add_callback(NULL, "reconfigure", do_reconfigure);

4. Settings outside the module block
------------------------------------

A module should keep its settings in its own block.  Reading core settings
is fine (conf_find_block(conf_root(), "serverinfo", NULL) and so on), but the core
variables already hold them.  A new top-level block is reported as unknown
by the core; to share settings between several modules, put them in a
nested block of one module's block, or in the block of the module the
others depend on.

The core binds its own tables with configure_block(blockname, label,
table, action), which binds a table to every top-level block with that
name and label; configure(modulename, table, action) is the same thing for
a module block, and is what the loader uses for module_config.  Both
return 0 on error, having reported it.  Nested blocks are not bound this
way; read them with the functions of section 3.
